---
import Chapter from './Chapter.astro';
import DropCap from '../ornament/DropCap.astro';
---
<Chapter n={2} title="Prologue" id="prologue">
  <div class="codex-measure prose pr-body" data-reveal-stagger>
    <p class="pr-first">
      <DropCap letter="I" /><span class="sr-only">I</span>&#8202;spend most of my time
      training models on images that doctors look at.
    </p>

    <p>
      My undergraduate thesis, <em>HierarchiRetina</em>, grades diabetic retinopathy
      severity from retinal photographs. It doesn&rsquo;t go straight from picture to
      grade. It screens first, then runs five separate segmentation models for five
      different things — microaneurysms, haemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton wool
      spots, vessels — then feeds those masks alongside the raw image into a grading
      model. The point is interpretability: when the system says &ldquo;severe,&rdquo;
      you can see which lesions drove that.
    </p>

    <p>
      Getting the data was its own project. I visited hospitals in person, emailed
      researchers and institutions, and went through more than 37 published papers.
      Every hospital request was declined. Most researcher emails were too. The FGADR
      authors wrote back, which is why FGADR is in the pipeline.
    </p>

    <p>
      Alongside the thesis I work with Niloy Farhan at BRAC on two problems. One is
      open-world species recognition: what does a model do when it meets an organism
      nobody trained it on, and where in the taxonomy does that organism belong? The
      other is measuring kidney length automatically from thick-slice CT, where slices
      sit 8mm apart and the kidney is tilted.
    </p>

    <p>
      The newest one has pulled me somewhere different. Instead of asking which genes
      correlate with Alzheimer&rsquo;s, it asks what would happen to a microglial cell
      if you knocked a specific gene down, and then checks that answer against CRISPR
      screens where somebody actually did knock it down.
    </p>

    <p>
      Next is an MSc in Computer Science and Engineering at BRAC University, starting
      Fall 2026. The proposal is already accepted. I want to keep doing research while
      I build a career in AI and machine learning, not choose between them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Outside computer science I read law and public policy, which turns out to be
      less of a detour than it sounds.
    </p>
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .pr-body { font-size: var(--body-size); }
  /* No clearfix: the drop cap floats on, and the paragraphs that follow
     wrap around it the way a printed page does. */
  .sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px; height: 1px;
    padding: 0; margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }
</style>
